          "page_text": "Using Pricing Cards in VCF Operations\nTechDocs\nLogin\n|\nRegister\nVMware Cloud Foundation 9.1.x\nCHANGE VERSION\n9.1\n9.0\nEnglish\nFrançais\nEspañol\n日本語\nOverview\nRelease Notes\nOverview\nDesign\nPlanning and Preparation\nDeployment, Convergence, and Upgrade\nLicensing\nBuilding Cloud Infrastructure\nvSphere\nvSAN\nNSX\nLifecycle Management\nFleet Management\nInfrastructure Operations\nWorkload Monitoring and Observability\nCost and Capacity Management\nConfiguring Cost\nCost Overview\nEditing Cost Drivers\nCost Analysis\nCost Settings\nOverview of Cost Drivers\nCloud Providers Overview\nCluster Cost Overview\nPublish Tag Based Cost as Individual Metrics\nUsing Pricing Cards in VCF Operations\nAdd New Pricing Card\nCost Calculation Status Overview\nCosting Enhancements\nPerforming VCF Automation Based Multitenancy in VCF Operations\nOptimizing Capacity\nWorkload Mobility\nSecurity and Compliance\nvSphere Supervisor Platform\nProvider Management\nOrganization Management\nWorkload Orchestration\nBuilding Cloud Applications\nPrivate AI\nAdministration SDKs, APIs, and CLI\nAdvanced Services\nDocumentation Legal Notice\nHome\nVMware Cloud Foundation 9.1.x\n...\nCost and Capacity Management\nConfiguring Cost\nUsing Pricing Cards in VCF Operations\nUsing Pricing Cards in VCF Operations\nYou can create pricing cards in\nVCF Operations\nto calculate the price associated with your virtual infrastructure. You can assign pricing cards to\nvCenter\ninstances or Clusters, depending on the pricing strategy. The pricing cards allows you to set the price for each resource present in your virtual environment.\nDownload PDF\n03 SEP | VCF 9.1.1 GA. For detailed dependencies and instructions on how to upgrade or patch, see\nVCF 9.1.1 Release Notes\n.\n12 OCT |\nNew patches for 9.1.1 components\nare now available.\nUse the latest available binaries for components when you deploy, upgrade, and patch.\nUsing Pricing Cards in VCF Operations\nYou can create pricing cards in\nVCF Operations\nto calculate the price associated with your virtual infrastructure. You can assign pricing cards to\nvCenter\ninstances or Clusters, depending on the pricing strategy. The pricing cards allows you to set the price for each resource present in your virtual environment.\nVCF Operations\nhas two types of pricing cards, rate-based pricing card and cost-based pricing card. After configuring a pricing card, you can assign it to one or more vCenters or Clusters as determined by the pricing strategy.\nYou can create pricing cards or you can migrate vCenter pricing cards to vCenter pricing policies. For details about creating a new pricing card, see\nAdd New Pricing Card\n.\nHow Is Price Calculated\nIn rate-based pricing policy\nVCF Operations\ncalculates the virtual infrastructure price based on the rate card. For rate-based pricing policy\nVCF Operations\nlets you define cost elements.\nVCF Operations\nrecalculates the price every 24-hours, the price calculation for the new pricing cards is done in the next price calculation cycle.\nHierarchy of Pricing Policy\nIn\nVCF Operations\n, policy is assigned to Clusters and/or\nvCenter\ninstances. The price is calculated for virtual machines, then aggregated and rolled up to vCenter. If there are two policies, a default policy for vCenter and another policy for Cluster, then the price calculation is based on the cluster policy for all the resources under the cluster. After that the cluster cost is rolled up to vCenter.\nWhen a virtual machine is under\nVCF Automation\nhierarchy and\nvCenter\nhierarchy, then the pricing is calculated based on the\nVCF Automation\nhierarchy and the virtual machine is removed from the vCenter resources and included under\nVCF Automation\nresources.\nConfiguring Cost\nAdd New Pricing Card\nPREVIOUS\nPublish Tag Based Cost as Individual Metrics\nNEXT\nAdd New Pricing Card\nWas this page helpful?\nDocumentation Feedback\nLast Updated October 15, 2026\nFeedback and Comments