          "excerpt": "UNITED ARAB EMIRATES: An Introduction to Regulatory: Government & Public Affairs Law | Chambers and Partners\nSkip to main content\nRankings\nRegional Rankings\nGlobal\nAsia-Pacific\nBrazil\nCanada\nEurope\nFrance\nGreater China Region\nLatin America\nUK\nUK Bar\nGermany\nUSA\nSpecialist Rankings\nNewLaw\nCrisis & Risk Management\nFinTech\nHigh Net Worth\nLitigation Support\nSpotlight Rankings\nUSA: Spotlight\nThought Leadership\nContent\nGlobal Practice Guides\nLegal Articles\nCustomer Voices\nChambers Content\nEvents\nRankings Dates\nEvents\nEvent Sponsorship\nProducts\nRankings\nProfile Platform\nBuild your brand, drive growth and amplify your ranking in the market\nPlaques\nShowcase your excellence\nIntelligence\nBusiness Intelligence\nTrack rankings performance to support growth decisions\nClient & Market Intelligence\nUnderstand what drives rankings success\nMarket Pulse\nLegal demand intelligence to guide growth decisions\nPractice Analytics\nNew\nBenchmark performance with real client feedback\nResponsible Business Intelligence\nResponsible business insights to drive measurable progress\nStrategic Intelligence\nNew\nLeadership intelligence for strategic decision-making\nTalent Intelligence\nTalent insights to strengthen attraction and retention\nShare your expertise\nPractice Guide\nShowcase your expertise and strengthen your brand with thought leadership for legal buyers\nIn-depth Overviews\nElevate your profile alongside Chambers rankings\nCareers in law\nChambers Student\nUK\nChambers Associate\nUSA\nDiscover what's new at Chamber",
          "page_text": "UNITED ARAB EMIRATES: An Introduction to Regulatory: Government & Public Affairs Law | Chambers and Partners\nSkip to main content\nRankings\nRegional Rankings\nGlobal\nAsia-Pacific\nBrazil\nCanada\nEurope\nFrance\nGreater China Region\nLatin America\nUK\nUK Bar\nGermany\nUSA\nSpecialist Rankings\nNewLaw\nCrisis & Risk Management\nFinTech\nHigh Net Worth\nLitigation Support\nSpotlight Rankings\nUSA: Spotlight\nThought Leadership\nContent\nGlobal Practice Guides\nLegal Articles\nCustomer Voices\nChambers Content\nEvents\nRankings Dates\nEvents\nEvent Sponsorship\nProducts\nRankings\nProfile Platform\nBuild your brand, drive growth and amplify your ranking in the market\nPlaques\nShowcase your excellence\nIntelligence\nBusiness Intelligence\nTrack rankings performance to support growth decisions\nClient & Market Intelligence\nUnderstand what drives rankings success\nMarket Pulse\nLegal demand intelligence to guide growth decisions\nPractice Analytics\nNew\nBenchmark performance with real client feedback\nResponsible Business Intelligence\nResponsible business insights to drive measurable progress\nStrategic Intelligence\nNew\nLeadership intelligence for strategic decision-making\nTalent Intelligence\nTalent insights to strengthen attraction and retention\nShare your expertise\nPractice Guide\nShowcase your expertise and strengthen your brand with thought leadership for legal buyers\nIn-depth Overviews\nElevate your profile alongside Chambers rankings\nCareers in law\nChambers Student\nUK\nChambers Associate\nUSA\nDiscover what's new at Chambers and Partners -\nclick here to find out more\nSubmissions\nSubmissions\nSubmissions Process\nMethodology\nResearch Schedule\nResearch Team\nSupport\nMy Account\nBack to Global Rankings\nUNITED ARAB EMIRATES: An Introduction to Regulatory: Government & Public Affairs\nContributors:\nHadef & Partners\nView Firm profile\nSeven Emirates, One Federation\nThe United Arab Emirates (\nUAE\n), formed in 1971, is a federation of seven emirates – Abu Dhabi, Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah, Umm Al Quwain and Ras Al Khaimah. While each retained its own cultural and economic characteristics, they united under a temporary constitution that outlined a framework for federal governance and collaboration. In 1996, the temporary constitution became permanent.\nOver the decades, the UAE has continually developed its legal system, implementing legislative, judicial and regulatory advancements that have transformed the country into a prominent global business hub, providing invaluable opportunities for a diversified economy that promotes both domestic and foreign investment.\nThe constitution of the UAE establishes both federal and emirate-level competencies, ensuring clarity, consistency and separation of powers between the federation and the emirates themselves. Articles 120, 121 and 122 of the UAE Constitution outline the matters that fall within the legislative and executive jurisdiction of the federation, as well as those within the jurisdiction of the individual emirates.\nThe federal government holds exclusive authority in areas deemed to be of national importance, such as foreign policy, defence, security, education, major commercial legislation, citizenship, air navigation and currency. This structure centralises decision-making on crucial matters and preserves a cohesive national identity. Each emirate maintains the right to enact laws on issues that concern its specific circumstances, such as municipal regulations, licensing of commercial activities and regulation of local economic initiatives. This preserves each emirate’s distinct character while contributing to the collective interest of the federation.\nArticle 151 of the UAE Constitution establishes the supremacy of the constitution, stating that it prevails over federal and emirate-level legislation, regulations and decisions. In cases of conflict between federal and emirate-level legislation, the inconsistent part of the emirate-level legislation is rendered null and void to the extent necessary to remove the inconsistency. Disputes regarding such conflicts are referred to the Federal Supreme Court for resolution.\nCivil law foundations and global Influence\nThe UAE’s legal landscape largely follows a civil law model, supplemented by international best practices. Over time, multiple influences, ranging from regional to global commercial norms, have impacted the UAE legal system.\nCentral to the UAE’s economic success is the establishment of free zones, which operate under bespoke business-friendly regulatory frameworks bolstering the UAE’s position as a global hub for businesses. These zones were formalised through various federal and emirate-level laws, allowing them to offer: (a) emirate-level tax incentives; (b) 100% foreign ownership; and (c) independent regulatory authority. Further, two major financial free zones have been established – the Dubai International Financial Centre (DIFC) and Abu Dhabi Global Market (ADGM) – which adopt common-law principles drawn primarily from English law. A Financial Free Zone is defined in Federal Law No 8 of 2004 as an independent jurisdiction within the UAE that is exempt from UAE federal civil and commercial laws, empowering DIFC and ADGM to create their own legal and regulatory frameworks for all civil and commercial matters. As a result, both DIFC and ADGM have their own bespoke regulatory framework and separate common law courts that adjudicate commercial and financial disputes.\nAs the UAE’s economy has diversified, multiple laws have been introduced or modernised to govern business transactions, corporate governance and commercial contracts. These laws draw on well-established international commercial and legal norms and continue to evolve to meet the expectations of international investors and provide a competitive advantage for foreign investment. Notably, the federal commercial companies law was amended in 2020 to allow 100% foreign ownership in most sectors across the UAE.\nAdditionally, in the last ten years, the UAE has introduced legislative reforms that strengthen its standing as a global centre for commerce and innovation. Reforms have been introduced in areas such as criminal law, cybercrime, data protection and privacy, labour, bankruptcy, technology, climate and environmental sustainability, fintech, blockchain, foreign direct investment, anti-money laundering and corporate tax. These initiatives align with the UAE’s broader strategy of encouraging foreign investment, nurturing innovation and maintaining a modern regulatory environment to ensure global competitiveness.\nEnactment of laws in the UAE\nLegislation at federal level is enacted through a clearly defined process aimed at ensuring thorough consultation and alignment with national priorities.\nNew laws are typically drafted under the direction of relevant ministries or federal authorities, and the draft is forwarded to the UAE Cabinet for initial approval. The Federal National Council (FNC), a consultative assembly comprising representatives from each emirate, examines draft laws, discusses their implications and proposes amendments. While the FNC’s role is advisory, its recommendations can significantly shape the proposed legislation. Once reviewed by the FNC, the draft law moves to the Federal Supreme Council (composed of the rulers of the seven emirates) for final endorsement. The President of the UAE signs the law, after which it is published in the Official Gazette. In urgent or exceptional circumstances, the UAE constitution allows the President, following consultation with the Cabinet, and with the endorsement of the Federal Supreme Council, to issue a Federal Decree-Law. Although it bypasses some steps of the standard legislative process for expediency, any such decree is presented to the FNC during its next session. Federal Decree-Laws carry the same legal force as other federal legislation once enacted.\nThe UAE's commitment to regional and international standards\nThe UAE is an active member of the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC), which facilitates economic, political and legislative cooperation among its member states. Harmonisation efforts often focus on standardising customs tariffs and VAT regulations to streamline cross-border commerce. The GCC has also developed a framework for the mutual recognition and enforcement of judicial rulings, making it easier for businesses to operate seamlessly within the GCC countries.\nThe UAE has entered into over 140 double taxation treaties to cooperate with other countries on tax matters. Additionally, it has adopted the Common Reporting Standard (CRS), an internationally agreed standard developed by the OECD to enhance tax transparency and combat tax evasion. By implementing the CRS, the UAE facilitates the automatic exchange of financial account information with other jurisdictions, promoting global tax compliance and fostering trust in its financial system.\nThe UAE has also entered into numerous bilateral investment treaties (BITs), which aim to attract and safeguard foreign investments. These treaties provide protections such as fair and equitable treatment, safeguards against expropriation and mechanisms for resolving disputes between investors and states. BITs play a critical role in creating a stable investment climate and enhancing the UAE's reputation as a reliable global investment hub.\nThe above underscores the UAE’s commitment to aligning its legal environment with broader regional and international objectives while retaining its own legislative autonomy.\nA legacy of adaptation and growth\nIn just over 50 years, the UAE has established a legal system that thrives on balancing local aspirations with global best practices. From the temporary constitution’s founding principles to the rise of free zones and specialised courts, the country’s ability to evolve has been crucial to its economic and reputational success.\nThrough its modernised regulatory framework and the strategic development of free zones, the UAE has cemented itself as a leading destination for international enterprises. Recent reforms in corporate governance, data privacy, taxation, environmental and labour regulations further underscore its commitment to staying at the forefront of global legal and commercial developments.\nBy continually modernising its legal ecosystem while respecting its federal framework and collaborating closely with GCC countries and international partners, the UAE remains well-positioned to support investors, entrepreneurs and global companies seeking a stable and forward-looking environment. This synergy of tradition, innovation, regional and international cooperation ensures that the UAE will remain a prominent global business hub for decades to come.\nAbout the firm:\nFirm OverviewIn a world of rapid change, understanding where you’re heading starts with analyzing where you’ve been. While some ...\nView more information about Hadef & Partners\nLegal Rankings\nFind A Law Firm\nFind A Lawyer\nTop Law Firms\nSubmissions\nSubmission Deadlines\nRankings Explained\nPractice Area Definitions\nOnboard With Chambers\nCompany\nContact Us\nFrequently Asked Questions\nModern Slavery Statement\nSupplier Code of Conduct\nCareers\nAbout Us\nDiversity, Equity and Inclusion\nNews & Media\nAffiliations\nPrivacy Policy\nAddress\nStreet\n165 Fleet Street\nCity\nLondon\nCountry\nUnited Kingdom\nPostal Code\nEC4A 2AE\nGoogle maps link\nView in maps\n© Copyright 2026 Chambers and Partners\n|\nTerms and Conditions",
