 share, and I think that will be when the next stock split occurs. There are several factors contributing to this, notably the fact that it's part of the\nDow Jones Industrial Average\n(DJINDICES: ^DJI) (which is weighted based on stock price versus market cap). There are a few stocks in the $800 or higher range that haven't split, but $1,000 is usually the point at which many companies start to consider a split.\nFor Nvidia to reach $1,000 per share, it would have to quadruple. Considering that Nvidia is already a $5.6 trillion company, that would be a huge rise. In fact, for Nvidia to get to $1,000 per share, it would need a market cap of over $24 trillion. That's a\nlong\nway off, and may cause investors to think that another stock split won't be coming, maybe ever.\nMissed AI's \"Act 1\"? Act 2 Could Be 14x Bigger.\nMost investors think they missed the AI boat because they didn't buy Nvidia in 2005. But according to our analysts, we're only at the end of \"Act 1\"—the R&D phase. \"Act 2\" is the global rollout.\nContinue »\nHowever, some projections suggest Nvidia could reach that level, maybe not in the not-too-distant future, like 2030. So, how will Nvidia get there? Let's do the math and find out.\nImage source: The Motley Fool.\nWhat kind of earnings does Nvidia need to be worth over $24 trillion?\nFirst, let's establish some parameters to assess Nvidia's ending valuation. Right now, Nvidia trades for about 29 times earnings, which is down quite a ways from where it traded at this time last year.\nNVDA PE Ratio\ndata by\nYCharts\nThe average\nS&P 500\ncompany trades at about 25.6 times earnings, so a price tag of 30 times earnings is probably pretty cheap. Still, it bakes some conservatism into the estimate. If it's something like 35 times earnings, Nvidia requires a lot less growth to get there.\nIf we take the average of the two and settle at 32.5 times trailing earnings, it would require Nvidia to produce $745 billion in earnings to obtain a $24.2 trillion valuation. Over the past 12 months, Nvidia's profit margin has reached 64%, which is slightly influenced by investment gains. Nvidia's non-GAAP profit margin excludes this, and in Q2 it was 56%. If we use a 55% profit margin, that would require $1.35 trillion in annual revenue.\nThat's a huge figure, but can Nvidia hit it?\nNvidia's path to $1,000 per share is a tough one\nStory Continues\nNvidia may not be as far from hitting that figure as one might think. This year, Wall Street analysts estimate Nvidia will generate $412 billion in revenue. Next year, that figure rises to $692 billion. So, by the end of 2027, it will be over halfway there. With Nvidia's revenue only needing to double in the time frame from 2028 to 2030, this projection just got a bit more palatable.\nNvidia has told investors that it expects global\nannual data\ncenter capital expenditures to reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by 2030. With the big five\nartificial intelligence\nhyperscalers expected to spend about $1.3 trillion next year, that leaves plenty of room for the marketplace to double between 2028 and 2030.\nSo, can Nvidia hit $1,000 per share? I think so, and it may happen as soon as 2030. It won't be an easy path to get there, and there may be bumps along the way, but if any stock can achieve it, I think it's Nvidia.\nShould you buy stock in Nvidia right now?\nBefore you buy stock in Nvidia, consider this:\nThe\nMotley Fool Stock Advisor\nanalyst team just identified what they believe are the\n10 best stocks\nfor investors to buy now… and Nvidia wasn't one of them. The 10 stocks that made the cut could produce monster returns in the coming years.\nConsider when\nNetflix\nmade this list on December 17, 2004... if you invested $1,000 at the time of our recommendation,\nyou'd have $379,123\n!* Or when\nNvidia\nmade this list on April 15, 2005... if you invested $1,000 at the time of our recommendation,\nyou'd have $1,408,822\n!*\nNow, it's worth noting\nStock Advisor's\ntotal average return is 950% — a market-crushing outperformance compared to 215% for the S&P 500. Don't miss the latest top 10 list, available with\nStock Advisor\n, and join an investing community built by individual investors for individual investors.\nSee the 10 stocks »\n*Stock Advisor returns as of October 12, 2026.\nKeithen Drury\nhas positions in Nvidia. The Motley Fool has positions in and recommends Nvidia. The Motley Fool has a\ndisclosure policy\n.\nPrediction: This Will Be Nvidia's Next Stock Split\nwas originally published by The Motley Fool\nTerms\nand\nPrivacy Policy\nPrivacy Dashboard\nMore Info\nRecommended Stories\nCopyright © 2026 Yahoo. All rights reserved.\nTrending Topics\nDow Jones\nS&P 500\nDAX Index\nNvidia\nTesla\nDJT\nTariffs\nExplore Further\nMortgages\nCredit Cards\nSectors\nCrypto Heatmap\nFinancial News\nAbout\nData Disclaimer\nHelp\nFeedback\nSitemap\nLicensing\nWhat's New\nAbout Our Ads\nTerms\nand\nPrivacy Policy\nPrivacy Dashboard\nMore Info\nADVERTISEMENT\nEdit your Dock\nMore News\nTerms\nand\nPrivacy Policy\nPrivacy Dashboard\nAd Terms\nFeedback\nMore Info",
