        {
          "evidence_id": "1d9adaa5-f46b-4cfb-b05c-5c898e875300",
          "source_type": "web_page",
          "source_title": "Google Trends",
          "source_url": "https://www.linkedin.com/company/google-trends",
          "publisher": "www.linkedin.com",
          "extracted_fact": "Welcome to the page dedicated to Google Trends updates, the powerful tool that tracks the latest search trends across the globe!",
          "excerpt": "Google Trends | LinkedIn\nSkip to main content\nLinkedIn\nTop Content\nPeople\nLearning\nJobs\nGames\nSign in\nJoin now\nGoogle Trends\nTechnology, Information and Internet\nFollow\nView all 2 employees\nReport this company\nAbout us\nWelcome to the page dedicated to Google Trends updates, the powerful tool that tracks the latest search trends across the globe! Here, we explore how trending topics are shaped, how they reflect global interests, and how businesses, marketers, and individuals can leverage real-time data to stay ahead of the curve.\n\nStay updated on:\n\nPopular Search Trends: Find out what people are searching for right now.\nIndustry Insights: Learn how trends are affecting different sectors.\nData-driven Marketing: Discover tips on using search trends for business growth.\nGlobal Events: See how world events impact what’s trending.\nThis page is not officially affiliated with Google, but is a community for anyone interested in how trends shape the digital world. Let’s explore the power of search together!\nWebsite\nhttps://trends.google.com/trends/\nExternal link for Google Trends\nIndustry\nTechnology, Information and Internet\nCompany size\n10,001+ employees\nType\nPublic Company\nFounded\n2006\nEmployees at Google Trends\nSabrina Elfarra\nNaya maura\nReporter at Smart Microsoft Word Powerpoint  skills for Documentation & Presentation\nSee all employees\nUpdates\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nMuhammad Rizwan\n2mo\nReport this post\nMost SEOs don’t need more AI tools.\n\nThey need fewer tools that actually ",
          "page_text": "Google Trends | LinkedIn\nSkip to main content\nLinkedIn\nTop Content\nPeople\nLearning\nJobs\nGames\nSign in\nJoin now\nGoogle Trends\nTechnology, Information and Internet\nFollow\nView all 2 employees\nReport this company\nAbout us\nWelcome to the page dedicated to Google Trends updates, the powerful tool that tracks the latest search trends across the globe! Here, we explore how trending topics are shaped, how they reflect global interests, and how businesses, marketers, and individuals can leverage real-time data to stay ahead of the curve.\n\nStay updated on:\n\nPopular Search Trends: Find out what people are searching for right now.\nIndustry Insights: Learn how trends are affecting different sectors.\nData-driven Marketing: Discover tips on using search trends for business growth.\nGlobal Events: See how world events impact what’s trending.\nThis page is not officially affiliated with Google, but is a community for anyone interested in how trends shape the digital world. Let’s explore the power of search together!\nWebsite\nhttps://trends.google.com/trends/\nExternal link for Google Trends\nIndustry\nTechnology, Information and Internet\nCompany size\n10,001+ employees\nType\nPublic Company\nFounded\n2006\nEmployees at Google Trends\nSabrina Elfarra\nNaya maura\nReporter at Smart Microsoft Word Powerpoint  skills for Documentation & Presentation\nSee all employees\nUpdates\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nMuhammad Rizwan\n2mo\nReport this post\nMost SEOs don’t need more AI tools.\n\nThey need fewer tools that actually improve the workflow.\n\nThe AI market is moving ridiculously fast.\n\nA tool that felt essential 6 months ago can become replaceable overnight.\n\nSo here’s how I’d rank the AI tools worth knowing for SEO, automation, content & programmatic workflows in 2026:\n\n🟢 TIER 1 - MUST USE\n\n☑ Windsurf → AI-assisted development workflows\n☑ Cursor → Powerful AI coding assistant\n☑ Claude Code → Strong coding + reasoning workflows\n☑ Perplexity AI → Fast research + source discovery\n☑ v0 by Vercel → Rapid UI generation\n☑ Lovable → Turn ideas into working products quickly\n\nThese become especially useful when SEO moves beyond keywords into:\n\nautomation → internal tools → landing pages → programmatic SEO.\n\n🟠 TIER 2 — AMAZING\n\n☑ ChatGPT → Research, analysis & ideation\n☑ Notion AI → Knowledge + workflow management\n☑ Gemini → Multimodal research\n☑ Replit → AI-assisted app building\n☑ Framer → Fast web experiences\n☑\nMake.com\n→ Workflow automation\n☑ Gamma AI → Rapid presentations\n☑ Julius AI → Data analysis\n\nNot mandatory for everyone.\n\nBut the right combination can save serious time.\n\n🟡 TIER 3 — RELIABLE\n\n☑ Surfer → Content optimization\n☑ Semrush → SEO research & monitoring\n☑ Ahrefs → Search + competitor intelligence\n☑ Grammarly → Writing refinement\n☑ Descript → Content editing\n☑ Arc Browser → Alternative browsing workflow\n\nStrong tools.\n\nBut not every SEO needs every one of them.\n\n🔵 TIER 4 - USE IF NEEDED\n\nGrok • Adobe Firefly • Poe • Manus • Character AI • Writesonic\n\nUseful in specific situations.\n\nNot necessarily core SEO tools.\n\n🔴 LOW PRIORITY\n\nMicrosoft Copilot • Meta AI\n\nThey may work for some teams, but I wouldn’t build my SEO stack around them first.\n\n⚫ MOVE ON / REPLACEABLE\n\nBing AI • IBM Watson • Jasper •\nYou.com\n•\nCopy.ai\nThe market has simply moved very fast.\n\nAnd that’s the bigger lesson:\n\nThe winner isn’t the person with 30 AI subscriptions.\n\nIt’s the person who can connect the right tools into one repeatable workflow.\n\nFor me, that workflow looks like:\n\nResearch → Build → Automate → Optimize → Measure\n\nDon’t collect AI tools.\n\nBuild a system.\n\n🔖 Save this before adding another AI subscription.\n\n♻️ Repost it for someone drowning in AI-tool overload.\n\nP.S. Which AI tool has actually become part of your daily SEO workflow?\n\nFollow\nMuhammad Rizwan\nfor practical insights on SEO, AEO, GEO, AI Search.\nView C2PA information\n48\n8 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nTayyaba Ameen\n2mo\nReport this post\nGoogle quietly built the most powerful SEO stack\n\n(And made it 100% FREE.)\n\nMost beginners never even open half of these tools.\nEvery serious SEO has them bookmarked.\n\nSave this before it slips off your feed 📌\n\n1. Google Search Console \nYour backstage pass to Google. \n↳ See exactly which keywords you rank for \n↳ Catch indexing problems before they tank traffic \n↳ Submit sitemaps in one click \n↳ Track clicks, impressions, CTR \n↳ Fix mobile issues fast\n\n2. Google Analytics 4 \nStop guessing what users do on your site. \n↳ Real traffic sources \n↳ Engagement + conversion tracking \n↳ Full user journey, start to exit \n↳ Pinpoint the page killing your funnel\n\n3. Google PageSpeed Insights \nSlow site = lost rankings. Period. \n↳ Mobile + desktop scores \n↳ Real-world field data \n↳ Core Web Vitals breakdown \n↳ Exact fixes, line by line\n\n4. Google Keyword Planner \nKeyword data straight from the source. \n↳ Real search volume \n↳ Low-competition gems \n↳ Related terms you'd never think of \n↳ Build content around actual demand\n\n5. Google Business Profile \nSkip this and your local SEO is invisible. \n↳ Show up on Maps \n↳ Collect reviews + answer Q&A \n↳ Post offers, photos, updates \n↳ Win local + voice searches\n\n6. Google Tag Manager \nNever wait on a developer again. \n↳ Add tracking scripts in minutes \n↳ Fire events without touching code \n↳ Build custom tracking setups \n↳ Keep your analytics clean\n\n7. Google Merchant Center \nA must if you sell products online. \n↳ Free product listings on Google \n↳ Auto-sync prices + promos \n↳ Plugs into Shopping ads \n↳ Huge boost to ecommerce visibility\n\n8. Google Trends \nSpot demand before everyone else. \n↳ Compare keyword interest over time \n↳ Catch seasonal spikes \n↳ Demand by location \n↳ Find content ideas worth chasing\n\n---\n\nThe truth?\n\nMaster these 8 first.\n\nEverything you need to start ranking is already in your hands.\n\n♻Save & Repost to help others too!\n\nPS: I help founders turn Reddit into their #1 lead channel\n\nFollow along for tips that work. DMs are open if you want real results.\n\nCheers, Tayyaba :)\n\n-\nTayyaba Ameen\n351\n75 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nChris Donnelly\n2mo\nReport this post\nOptimising your landing page for rankings is not enough.\n\nWhat you need to know is whether it shows in AI answers: \n\nThat is the seismic shift I've been harping on about for the better part of a year.\n\nWhen someone asks AI for a product or service recommendation...\nYour site will either be cited & recommended, or completely ignored.\n\nThe good news is a lot of AEO is supplemented by SEO fundamentals. \n\nBut when it comes to GEO, it's a completely different ball game.\nEven every AI model has a different playbook.\n\nHowever, when it comes to your website,\nThere are non-negotiables you can't miss across the board.\n\nMost sites I see are honestly painful to look at, knowing what I know...\n\nSo, these are the sections you fundamentally can't miss:\n\n1. The H1\nThe question your buyer would type, answered in the same line.\n\n2. The byline\nA named author with photo, credentials and a bio link, so a real person stands behind the page.\n\n3. The subheadline\nOne line naming the outcome they walk away with.\n\n4. Question sub-headings\nEvery H2 and H3 as a real question, so AI can lift a clean answer from each.\n\n5. The answer block\nA 40 to 60 word answer to the main question, up top, above the fold.\n\n6. The build\nRaw HTML, copy as text, alt text on images, transcript under the video. Nothing hidden from crawlers.\n\n7. The problem block\nName the problem in their words, then show what it's costing them.\n\n8. The FAQ block\nReal questions as headers, clean answers, marked up so AI pulls them straight in.\n\n9. The solution block\nHow you solve it, led by the outcome, closing on one clear next step.\n\n10. Schema in the head\nOrganisational schema for your brand, FAQ, Product and Article schema on the page.\n\n11. The proof block\nNamed case studies with real, checkable numbers, plus any original data you own.\n\n12. The comparison table\nA table of features and pricing versus the alternatives, easy for AI to quote.\n\n13. The pricing CTA\nOne action button in plain words, \"start free\" or \"book a demo\".\n\n14. The last-updated stamp\nA visible last-updated date and fresh schema, so AI keeps treating the page as live.\n\nYou can see that Search is just more advanced than it used to be.\n\nNot to say it was ever *easy* necessarily...\nBut it is definitely harder and more complex now.\n\nIf your site isn't structured like this, it won't show up where it counts.\n\nThis is the shift we saw well ahead of time at\nSearchable\n.\n\n📌 For free, you can use our AI SEO Agent to optimise your page:\nhttps://lnkd.in/gVwCJJDX\nHave fun optimising !\n\n♻️ Repost to help your network get ahead of the shift.\nAnd follow\nChris Donnelly\nfor more AI SEO guidance.\n2,599\n598 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nEmilia Möller\n2mo\nReport this post\n93.05% of commercial Google searches now trigger an AI Overview.\n\nNot long ago, that number was around 6%.\n\nI don't think anyone, including Google, expected it to move this fast.\n\nAnd it matters because of which searches are changing. Commercial, high-intent searches are increasingly triggering AI Overviews, while navigational searches often aren't.\n\nThat's the moment someone is comparing options and deciding who to trust. If your brand isn't part of that answer, you're invisible before they've even clicked a website.\n\nThat doesn't mean SEO is dead. It means SEO isn't enough on its own anymore.\n\nThe way I think about it is simple: \n\n→ SEO gets you discovered. \n→ AEO gets you recommended. \n→ GEO helps AI understand and trust your brand.\n\nHere's what every marketer should be paying attention to:\n\n1️⃣ Rankings are no longer the whole story.\nRanking #1 doesn't guarantee visibility anymore. Google is increasingly summarising the options instead of sending users to ten blue links. The brands that get cited become part of the conversation before a click ever happens.\n\n2️⃣ Commercial intent is where the biggest shift is happening.\nAI Overviews aren't showing up equally across search. They're dominating high-intent commercial queries. That's where the revenue is, which also means that's where businesses have the most to lose.\n\n3️⃣ Search hasn't died. It's evolved.\nTechnical SEO, authority and great content still matter. But now you also need to optimise for how AI systems retrieve, understand and recommend information. The businesses adapting early are building an advantage that's going to become harder to catch every month.\n\nIf you want to get out ahead of this, you're still on time. \n\nBut you need an audit to know exactly where the gaps are. \n\nThat's why we built a free AI Visibility Report. \n\nIt shows you where you need to start across the top 4 AI tools. \n\nClick here to grab your free assessment:\nhttps://lnkd.in/d3-HxsU2\n♻️ Repost to help marketers understand the new era of search. \n🔔 Follow\nEmilia Möller\nfor more on AI search and building with AI.\n327\n153 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nMuhammad Rizwan\n2mo\nReport this post\nMost SEO problems don’t need another complicated workflow.\n\nThey need the right tool at the right moment.\n\nOne tool to find indexing issues.\n\nOne to diagnose slow pages.\n\nOne to uncover keyword opportunities.\n\nAnd one to understand what competitors are doing better.\n\nSo I organized 25 free SEO tools and useful free tiers by the job they help you complete:\n\n🔵 GOOGLE ESSENTIALS\n\n1. Google Search Console - monitor indexing and search performance\n2. Google Analytics 4 - track organic traffic and conversions\n3. Google Trends - identify rising topics and search demand\n4. Google Keyword Planner - discover keyword ideas and volume ranges\n5. Looker Studio - turn SEO data into shareable dashboards\n\n🔴 TECHNICAL & PERFORMANCE\n\n6. PageSpeed Insights - check Core Web Vitals and page speed\n7. Lighthouse - audit performance, accessibility and SEO\n8. Rich Results Test - test eligibility for Google rich results\n9. Schema Markup Validator - validate structured data\n10. GTmetrix - diagnose page-speed problems\n\n🟢 AUDITING & INDEXING\n\n11. Screaming Frog SEO Spider - crawl up to 500 URLs\n12. Ahrefs Free - audit and analyze verified websites\n13. Bing Webmaster Tools - review Bing indexing and SEO reports\n14. SEOquake - access instant page-level SEO metrics\n15. Detailed SEO Extension - inspect important on-page elements\n\n🟡 KEYWORDS & CONTENT\n\n16. AnswerThePublic - discover questions people are searching for\n17. Keyword Surfer - view search-volume data inside Google\n18. Ubersuggest - generate keyword and content ideas\n19. Semrush Free - perform limited SEO research and audits\n20. Moz Keyword Explorer - explore keyword opportunities\n\n🟣 LINKS, COMPETITORS & LOCAL\n\n21. Ahrefs Backlink Checker - preview backlinks and authority\n22. Moz Link Explorer - analyze limited link metrics\n23. Similarweb Extension - estimate traffic and competitor activity\n24. Google Business Profile - manage local search visibility\n25. PlePer Local SEO Tools - perform GBP and local SEO checks\n\nSome are completely free. \n\nOthers include limited searches, reports or optional upgrades, but their free access is still genuinely useful.\n\nThe goal isn’t to use all 25 every day.\n\nIt’s to build a focused stack around your workflow.\n\nIf you’re starting, begin with:\n\n→ Google Search Console\n→ Google Analytics 4\n→ PageSpeed Insights\n→ Screaming Frog\n→ Keyword Surfer\n→ Ahrefs Backlink Checker\n\nTogether, these tools can help you move from:\n\nIndexing → performance → auditing → keywords → backlinks.\n\nMaster these six first.\n\nThen add another tool only when a specific task demands it.\n\nA small stack you understand deeply will always beat a browser full of tools you rarely use.\n\nWhich free SEO tool has become essential to your workflow?\n\nAnd which one deserves a place on this list? 👇\n\n❤️ Save this toolkit for your next SEO project.\n\n♻️ Repost it to help another marketer build a smarter SEO workflow.\n\n➕ Follow for practical SEO tools, systems and workflows.\n87\n10 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nApoorva Mathe\n2mo\nReport this post\n22 SEO Chrome extensions (updated):\n\n1. Detailed SEO\n2. SEOquake\n3. SEO Minion\n4. OnPageSEOai\n5. Redirect Path\n6. META SEO Inspector\n7. Lighthouse\n8. Schema Builder\n9. MozBar\n10. Majestic Backlink Analyzer\n11. BuzzStream Buzzmarker\n12. Hunter\n13. Similarweb\n14. Ahrefs SEO Toolbar\n15. Serpstat\n16. SerpWorx\n17. Keyword Surfer\n18. Keywords Everywhere\n19. SEO Search Keyword Tool\n20. GMB Everywhere\n21. ProfilePro\n22. Phantom\n\nI had one problem.\n\nMy browser felt slow.\n\nI used random extensions.\n\nNothing stayed useful.\n\nThen I made one list.❤️\n\nMy marketing work improved.\n\nYou need fewer extensions.\n\nNot every extension matters.\n\nThe right strategy matters.\n\nReal growth needs practice.\n\nUse what fits you.\n\nLearn one step-by-step.\n\nResults follow action. \n\nSave this post for later.\n\n♻️Repost to inspire other.\n\nFollow\nApoorva Mathe\nfor more AI SEO tips.\n\nWork with me:\nhttps://lnkd.in/gARiejFc\nP.S. Which one extensions helps you?😊\n589\n72 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nMuhammad Rizwan\n2mo\nReport this post\nMy SEO workflow used to look productive.\n\nIn reality, it was mostly tab switching.\n\nRankings in one tool.\n\nTraffic in another.\n\nBacklinks somewhere else.\n\nCrawl issues in another report.\n\nContent ideas scattered across different tabs.\n\nI had plenty of data, but no single place connecting the dots.\n\nThat changed when I stopped treating Claude like another writing tool.\n\nI started using it as the intelligence layer at the center of my SEO workflow. ❤️\n\nHere are 8 SEO connectors worth building around Claude:\n\n1. Semrush - keyword, competitor and site-audit insights\n2. Ahrefs - backlinks, organic research and SERP opportunities\n3. Google Search Console - clicks, impressions, CTR and rankings\n4. Google Analytics 4 - traffic, engagement and conversions\n5. Screaming Frog - crawl data and technical SEO issues\n6. Moz - authority metrics, keywords and on-page insights\n7. Surfer SEO - content briefs and optimization signals\n8. BrightEdge - enterprise rankings, visibility and reporting\n\nBut the real value isn’t simply connecting more tools.\n\nIt’s what becomes possible when their data starts working together.\n\nImagine this workflow:\n\n→ Pull declining queries from Search Console\n→ Add GA4 engagement and conversion data\n→ Review technical issues from Screaming Frog\n→ Compare keyword gaps using Semrush or Ahrefs\n→ Ask Claude to connect the patterns and prioritize the next actions\n\nNow you can ask better questions:\n\n“Which pages are losing traffic despite stable rankings?”\n\n“Where do we have authority but weak content?”\n\n“Which technical fixes could create the biggest organic impact?”\n\n“What should the SEO team prioritize this week, and why?”\n\nThat is the shift.\n\nClaude doesn’t replace your SEO tools.\n\nIt helps you understand their data together, so you can move from scattered reports to clearer decisions.\n\nAnd you don’t need to connect all eight on day one.\n\nStart with the tool containing your most valuable data.\n\nFor most websites, that will be Google Search Console.\n\nConnect one source.\n\nAsk one important question.\n\nBuild one repeatable workflow.\n\nThen add another connector when it gives Claude the missing context needed to produce a better answer.\n\nA smaller connected system will always outperform a larger stack of disconnected dashboards.\n\nWhich SEO tool would you connect to Claude first?\n\nMore importantly, what would you ask it? 👇\n\n❤️ Save this workflow for later.\n\n♻️ Repost it to help another SEO professional reduce tab chaos.\n\n➕ Follow me for practical AI SEO systems and workflows.\n219\n30 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nSunny Grewal\n2mo\nReport this post\n30 Modern marketing AI tools(updated):\n\n1. Measure & Win\n2. Strategy & Intelligence\n3. Content Creation & Optimization\n4. Distribution & Engagement\n5. Automation & Workflows\n6. Analytics & Reporting\n\nI tested tools without a clear plan.\n\nMy problem was simple.\n\nToo many tools.\n\nNo clear system.\n\nSo I made one change.❤️\n\nI grouped every tool.\n\nBy its main job.\n\nNow I know what each tool does.\n\nYou don't need 30 random AI tools.\n\nYou need the right tool.\n\nFor the right task.\n\nAI can help you. \n\nCreate. Plan. Automate. Measure results.\n\nSave this post for later.\n\n♻️Repost to inspire other.\n\nFollow\nSunny Grewal\nfor more AI SEO tips.\n\nWork with me:\nhttps://lnkd.in/d5YjTrQW\nImage credit:\nChris Donnelly\nP.S. What tool would you add?😊\n266\n151 Comments\nLike\nComment\nShare\nGoogle Trends\nreposted this\nMadhav Mistry\n2mo\nReport this post\n\"Using AI\" is NOT modern marketing.\n\"Posting daily\" is NOT modern marketing.\n\"Launching ads\" is NOT modern marketing.\n\nMost marketers confuse tools with strategy.\n\nBecause of this confusion, they optimize activities rather than build a marketing system.\n\n- Whom are we helping?\n- Where are they discovering us?\n- Why should they trust us?\n\nAnd how do we stay visible?\n\nAs a result, they get easily distracted:\n\n\"Let's try this new AI tool!\"\n\"Everyone is talking about this platform!\"\n\"We don't want to miss this trend!\"\n\nAnd this lack of focus puts marketing into a reactive posture:\n\nNo clear strategy → A new trend appears → Reaction → \n\"Let's try this new tactic!\" → Even less strategic focus.\n\nTo build modern marketing, teams need to distinguish strategy from execution:\n\n1. Strategy comes first: It defines the customer journey, positioning, messaging, and business goals.\n\n2. Tools come second: They help execute the strategy faster, smarter, and at scale.\n\nI've tested 20+ tools, including\nSemrush\n, Canva, Adobe,\nZapier\n, Limy AI, Gamma, Replit, Meta Ads, and many more.\n\nEach one makes me more efficient.\n\nNone of them replaces strategy.\n\nNow here's one last important note:\nThe best marketers don't win because they use more tools.\n\nThey win because they know which tools to use, when to use them, and how to connect them into one marketing system.\n\n♻️ Repost to share with your network\nFollow\nMadhav Mistry\nfor insights on Marketing!\n407\n18 Comme",
