          "page_text": "Intel Stock Has Nearly Tripled in 2026. A $10,000 Stake From 2016 Still Trails the S&P 500.\nOops, something went wrong\nSkip to navigation\nSkip to main content\nSkip to right column\nYahoo Finance\nHome\nNews\nFinance\nSports\nMore\nNews\nUS\nPolitics\nWorld\nTech\nScience\nOriginals\nWeather\nNewsletters\nFinance\nMy portfolio\nNews\nMarkets\nResearch\nCommunity\nPersonal finance\nVideos\nSports\nFantasy Football 2026\nNFL\nCollege football\nNBA\nMLB\nNHL\nCollege basketball\nSoccer\nFantasy\nSports Betting\nNFL Odds\nYahoo Sports AM\nShopping\nStyle\nBeauty\nHealth\nHome and garden\nTech\nGift ideas\nStores\nShopping Guides\nDeals\nToys and games\nShopping lists\nGeneral\nEntertainment\nCelebrity\nTV\nMovies\nMusic\nHow to Watch\nVideos\nNonfiction Hotlist\nGames\nLife\nHealth\nParenting\nPets\nHoroscopes\nFood\nTravel\nAutos\nLocal\nSelected edition\nUS\nEnglish\nSign in\nMy Portfolio\nMarkets\nStocks\nMost Active\nDay Gainers\nDay Losers\nTrending\nHighest Dividend\nLarge Cap\nSmall Cap\nMost Expensive\nHighest Beta\nPink Sheet\nUnusual Volume\nCrypto\nMost Active\nDay Gainers\nDay Losers\nPrivate Companies\n52 Week Gainers\nRecently Funded\nMost Funded\nOptions\nDay Gainers\nDay Losers\nHighest Open Interest\nHighest Implied Volatility\nSectors\nTechnology\nFinancial Services\nConsumer Cyclical\nConsumer Defensive\nCommunication Services\nHealthcare\nIndustrials\nTreasury Bonds\nCurrencies\nWorld Indices\nCommodities\nETFs\nMutual Funds\nNews\nMarkets\nStock Market News\nReal Estate\nMagnificent 7\nPrivate Markets\nFutures & Derivatives\nOriginals\nNewsletters\nEconomy & Policy\nEarnings\nOil & Gas\nStrait of Hormuz\nTariffs\nClean Energy\nTech\nAerospace & Defense\nEnterprise Cloud\nExchange-Traded Funds\nResearch & Tools\nCalendars\nEarnings Calendar\nIPO Calendar\nStock Splits\nEconomic Events\nScreeners\nEquities\nMost Shorted\nMost Active Penny Stocks\nAnalyst Strong Buy\nStock Comparison\nCurrency Converter\nAdvanced Charts\nCommunity\nPersonal Finance\nMy Money\nBETA\nCredit Cards\nCompare Credit Cards\nBest 0% APR Cards\nBest Balance Transfer\nBest Cash Back Cards\nBest Travel Cards\nBanking\nCD Rates\nBest High-Yield Savings\nBest Money Market\nBest Free Checking\nBest Online Banks\nMortgage\nBest Mortgage Lenders\nBest Mortgage Rates\nMortgage Refinance\nMortgage Calculator\nMortgage Affordability Calculator\nStudent Loans\nFederal vs. Private\nFAFSA Guide\nStudent Loan Assistance\nStudent Loan Forgiveness\nPaying Off Student Loans\nPersonal Loans\nBest Personal Loans\nGet Approved\nBest Lenders\nInsurance\nFind Car Insurance\nTypes of Car Insurance\nCar Insurance Discounts\nFind Life Insurance\nTaxes\nHow to File\nRefund Status\nTax Extension\nChild Tax Credit\nInvesting\nInvesting Basics\nReal Estate Investing\nRetirement Basics\nCalculators\nWatch\nLive\nDaily Shows\nThe 8:30\nSozzi Unleashed\nThe Daily Wolf\nMarket Hang\nIn the Loop\nWeekly Shows\nBuilt Different\nHow They Run\nPower Players\nThe Big Idea\nTrader Talk\nETF Report\nFA Corner\nMore to Watch\nMust-watch Interviews\nMag 7\nFed Watch\nCrypto\nAI\nNetwork\nU.S. markets close in 3h 17m\nUS Markets\nUS Markets\nEurope Markets\nAsia Markets\nCryptocurrencies\nRates\nCommodities\nCurrencies\nU.S. markets close in 3h 17m\nS&P 500\n7,690.75\n-53.31\n-0.69%\nDow 30\n52,089.30\n-88.39\n-0.17%\nNasdaq\n26,319.21\n-393.82\n-1.47%\nRussell 2000\n2,737.50\n-29.77\n-1.08%\n10-Yr Bond\n5.13\n+0.04\n+0.75%\nVIX\n17.23\n+0.77\n+4.68%\nGold\n4,185.10\n+15.00\n+0.36%\nBitcoin USD\n81,391.86\n-630.69\n-0.77%\nCrude Oil Nov 26\n97.97\n+1.47\n+1.52%\nUS Private Equity\n10,942.28\n+14.10\n+0.13%\nADVERTISEMENT\nIntel Stock Has Nearly Tripled in 2026. A $10,000 Stake From 2016 Still Trails the S&P 500.\nDaniel Sparks, The Motley Fool\nWed, October 14, 2026 at 8:03 AM GMT+3\n5 min read\nINTC\n^GSPC\nNVDA\nVOO\nShares of\nIntel\n(NASDAQ:INTC) trade near $101 as of this writing, up about 175% in 2026. The company's stock is also worth over five times the $18 or so it closed at in April 2025 -- its lowest close in the last decade.\nBut zoom out to 10 years, and the picture isn't nearly as pretty. A $10,000 investment in\nIntel\nin mid-October 2016, with all dividends reinvested, is worth around $33,700 now. The same $10,000 in the\nVanguard S&P 500 ETF\n(NYSEMKT:VOO), an index fund that tracks the\nS&P 500\n(SNPINDEX:^GSPC), turned into about $42,900.\nMissed Nvidia in 2009? This Rare Signal Is Flashing Again.\nIn 2009, a \"Double Down\" signal flashed for a little-known chipmaker called Nvidia. For the first time in years, that same \"Total Conviction\" signal is flashing for a company 1/100th the size of Nvidia.\nContinue »\nSo even after nearly tripling this year, Intel stock still trails just owning the market. And I think the reasons help show how much of the turnaround investors are already paying for.\nImage source: Intel. Image source: Intel.\nNine years of next to nothing\nIntel closed just above $37 on Oct. 12, 2016, and it finished 2025 at $36.90. In short, the share price went nowhere for over nine years, and dividends were the only return shareholders had to show for it.\nThe stock wasn't always behind. At the end of 2019, the $10,000 stake was worth around $17,500, ahead of the index fund's $16,100 or so. But by the end of 2022, it had dropped to about $8,500, while the same amount in the fund had doubled to about $20,100. At the stock's bottom in April 2025, the Intel stake was down to around $6,000. And when 2025 ended, it was worth about $12,300, versus about $37,300 for the fund.\nSo around 90% of Intel's 10-year gain has come in under 10 months.\nThe business went backward over that span, too. Intel's\nrevenue\nrose from $59.4 billion in 2016 to a record $79.0 billion in 2021 -- and then slid to $52.9 billion in 2025, less than the company pulled in nine years before.\nThe dividend is gone\nDuring most of the decade, a rising dividend at least lifted shareholders' returns. Intel's quarterly payout topped out at $0.365 per share in 2022. But the company cut it to $0.125 in February 2023 and suspended it entirely starting with the fourth quarter of 2024.\nThose reinvested dividends still make up around $6,400 of today's $33,700. Without them, the $10,000 would be worth about $27,300.\nThe index fund, of course, still pays one, yielding around 1%. Intel pays nothing, and I doubt that changes soon.\nStory Continues\nThe stock is priced for far higher profits\nYes, the business is improving fast. Intel's second-quarter revenue climbed 25% year over year to $16.1 billion, accelerating from 7% growth in the first quarter. And non-GAAP (adjusted) earnings per share came in at $0.42, versus a loss of $0.10 a year earlier. That one quarter matched the $0.42 Intel earned in all of 2025, which followed an adjusted loss of $0.13 a share in 2024.\nBut the Intel of 2016 earned much more. Adjusted earnings per share were $2.72 that year. Even four quarters like the second quarter would come to just $1.68, and management's July outlook for the third quarter (which Intel reports on Oct. 29) was $0.38.\nWhat jumps out at me is how much more investors now pay for those earnings. At around $37 in mid-October 2016, shares traded at about 14 times that year's adjusted earnings. Today, buyers are paying about 48 times the earnings expected for 2027.\nThis year's run, then, arguably has less to do with what Intel earns today than with what investors expect it to earn a few years out. If those profits show up late or come in lower, the stock might lose a big part of its gains.\nFor the 10-year stake to draw even with the index fund, shares would have to climb about 27%, to roughly $129. They've been there before. Intel set a record close of $140.94 on June 22, when the stake was briefly ahead of the fund, and the stock is down 28% since.\nOverall, I think Intel's turnaround is showing up in its numbers. But the stock price seems to assume years of further progress, and Intel shareholders have already seen how long the wait can be when the business stumbles.\nWith shares priced at around 48 times 2027's expected earnings and paying no dividend, I'd rather own the index fund here.\nShould you buy stock in Intel right now?\nBefore you buy stock in Intel, consider this:\nThe\nMotley Fool Stock Advisor\nanalyst team just identified what they believe are the\n10 best stocks\nfor investors to buy now… and Intel wasn't one of them. The 10 stocks that made the cut could produce monster returns in the coming years.\nConsider when\nNetflix\nmade this list on December 17, 2004... if you invested $1,000 at the time of our recommendation,\nyou'd have $380,795\n!* Or when\nNvidia\nmade this list on April 15, 2005... if you invested $1,000 at the time of our recommendation,\nyou'd have $1,390,634\n!*\nNow, it's worth noting\nStock Advisor's\ntotal average return is 946% — a market-crushing outperformance compared to 215% for the S&P 500. Don't miss the latest top 10 list, available with\nStock Advisor\n, and join an investing community built by individual investors for individual investors.\nSee the 10 stocks »\n*Stock Advisor returns as of October 14, 2026.\nDaniel Sparks\nand his clients do not have positions in any of the stocks mentioned. The Motley Fool has positions in and recommends Intel and Vanguard S&P 500 ETF. The Motley Fool has a\ndisclosure policy\n.\nIntel Stock Has Nearly Tripled in 2026. A $10,000 Stake From 2016 Still Trails the S&P 500.\nwas originally published by The Motley Fool\nTerms\nand\nPrivacy Policy\nPrivacy Dashboard\nMore Info\nRecommended Stories\nCopyright © 2026 Yahoo. All rights reserved.\nTrending Topics\nDow Jones\nS&P 500\nDAX Index\nNvidia\nTesla\nDJT\nTariffs\nExplore Further\nMortgages\nCredit Cards\nSectors\nCrypto Heatmap\nFinancial News\nAbout\nData Disclaimer\nHelp\nFeedback\nSitemap\nLicensing\nWhat's New\nAbout Our Ads\nTerms\nand\nPrivacy Policy\nPrivacy Dashboard\nMore Info\nADVERTISEMENT\nEdit your Dock\n